 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Bosch WTN85201GB condenser dryer not drying properly anymore, clothes still damp after 2 hrs. any ideas"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Poor drying on a Bosch heat-pump dryer is most often caused by restricted airflow. The two main things to check are the lint filter in the door opening and the condenser unit behind the bottom flap; if either is clogged with fluff, the dryer cannot circulate air effectively. Please ensure both are clean before proceeding. It's also worth confirming that your washing machine spun the clothes well (high spin speed) before they went into the dryer, as very wet laundry takes much longer to dry."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ok, I'll check the door filter and the condenser. Spin is 1200 and a normal load. How do I get at the condenser?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "To access the condenser on your Bosch WTN85201GB, pull down the small flap at the bottom right of the front panel. The condenser unit is a vertical rectangular block sitting behind it; simply pull it straight out towards you. Let it drip-dry for about 10 minutes before sliding it back into place and running your cycle again. Did you find a significant amount of fluff or blockage inside it?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Pulled it out - the front face was matted with lint, never cleaned it before. Rinsed it with the shower head and it came clean. Door filter had fluff and a waxy film too, scrubbed it under the tap. Anything else to check before I run a cycle?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "That sounds like the root cause. A heavily blocked condenser and lint filter will severely restrict airflow, which is exactly why your clothes were coming out damp. You might also want to wipe the metal sensor strips inside the drum with a bit of vinegar if you use fabric conditioner, as that can affect drying accuracy. Run a cycle and let me know how it goes."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Wiped the sensor bars with vinegar too, they were a bit dull. Ran a cycle and the clothes dried in about 100 minutes, and the water container is nearly full now."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "That is excellent news. To prevent this from happening again, I would suggest checking the lint filter and condenser every few cycles rather than waiting for them to get completely blocked. If you need anything else, just let me know."
  }
 ],
